{
  "entity0": {
    "rationale": "Iteration 2 moved the seven-sensor row from y = 0.03 m to y = 0.05 m, and the result was worse on the measures that matter. Line losses returned: 1.0 per run on the slalom and 0.2 on the s-bend, where iteration 1 had zero. Oval max_error rose from 0.0613 m to 0.0637 m, and slalom max_error reached 0.0658 m. Progress also fell on the oval (0.205 to 0.193) and slalom (0.098 to 0.100 with an earlier loss). The forward lead therefore hurt rather than helped. The Integrator's rule for this case is that line losses on the slalom or s-bend mean the span is too narrow for the tape at the offsets reached, so widen it. I make two geometry changes. First, I revert the row to y = 0.03 m, the position where iteration 1 kept the line with zero losses. Second, I widen the span to x = ±0.08 m with seven sensors at 0.0267 m spacing, the largest allowed span. The 16 mm footprint leaves gaps of about 10.7 mm, which is less than the 25 mm tape, so at least one sensor always sees tape while the line is inside the span. I keep the 0.6 kg mass, 0.12 m wheel track, 0.03 m wheel radius and 0.12 m body width, because iteration 1 showed no sign that they limit the result, and the 0.06 m off-track floor means body width cannot help. The Integrator's controller changes (higher ki, with kp and kd held) stay theirs to set. The expected result is zero line losses on all tracks, with max_error in the 0.061 m range and the residual error driven by controller bias rather than sensing. A completed run is still unlikely unless max_error drops below 0.06 m. If line losses stay at zero and max_error stays near 0.061 m, geometry is not the lever and the next change is in the controller.",
    "geometry": {
      "body_length": 0.18,
      "body_width": 0.12,
      "mass": 0.6,
      "sensor_positions": [
        {"x": -0.08, "y": 0.03},
        {"x": -0.0533, "y": 0.03},
        {"x": -0.0267, "y": 0.03},
        {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.03},
        {"x": 0.0267, "y": 0.03},
        {"x": 0.0533, "y": 0.03},
        {"x": 0.08, "y": 0.03}
      ],
      "sensor_size": 0.016,
      "wheel_radius": 0.03,
      "wheel_track": 0.12
    },
    "intent": "Remove the line losses that returned in iteration 2 by reverting the sensor row to y = 0.03 m, the lead at which iteration 1 had zero losses, and widening the span to ±0.08 m so the tape stays in view at the offsets reached on the slalom and s-bend. Keep the 16 mm footprint, 0.6 kg mass and drivetrain fixed so the change can be attributed to the sensor span and row position. Expected result: line_loss_events of 0 on all three development tracks, and max_error at or near 0.061 m. The Integrator should rescale the sensor weights to the wider span (about x*25 instead of x*33).",
    "feedback_request": {
      "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "max_error", "rms_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation"],
      "concrete_requests": [
        "Report line_loss_events per track, and the step of the first loss if any occur.",
        "Report max_error per track and whether it falls below 0.06 m on the oval and s-bend.",
        "Report steering_oscillation on the slalom, to check whether the wider span with 16 mm footprints adds jitter.",
        "Report the sum of sensor readings on the slalom at the outermost sensors, to confirm the outer sensors reach the tape without saturating."
      ],
      "questions": [
        "With the row back at y = 0.03 m and span ±0.08 m, are line losses zero on the slalom and s-bend?",
        "If max_error is still about 0.061 m with zero losses, is the controller bias (ki) still the limit rather than geometry?",
        "Does the wider span with 16 mm footprints (gap about 10.7 mm) produce a noisy centre signal?"
      ]
    },
    "learning": "Iteration 2 showed that moving the sensor row forward to y = 0.05 m brought back line losses (1.0 on the slalom, 0.2 on the s-bend) and raised oval max_error to 0.0637 m. The forward lead is not a lever for this robot at its current gains, so I revert it. The earlier evidence still holds: line losses are a span problem, and the ±0.06 m span was too narrow at the offsets reached on the slalom. Widening to ±0.08 m is the next test.",
    "rationale_note": "Revert row to y=0.03; widen span to ±0.08 (seven sensors)."
  },
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Revert row, widen to ±0.08",
      "notes": "Moves the sensor row back to y = 0.03 m and widens the seven-sensor span to ±0.08 m, because iteration 2's forward row brought back line losses (1.0 on the slalom, 0.2 on the s-bend) and raised oval max_error to 0.0637 m. Expected result is zero line losses, with max_error near 0.061 m if the remaining limit is controller bias."
    }
  }
